There's two different ways
to export your billing data.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.290
First is through BigQuery.

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.185
This would be my
preferred option.

00:12:13.185 --> 00:12:15.310
It means that you can do
more with that data that's

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:16.450
been exported.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.000
You can also export
your data as a CSV.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.530
To export the data in BigQuery,
it is an automatic every day

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:27.130
sync between your budget
data and BigQuery.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:30.650
So what I do is simply
hit this Edit Settings,

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:34.070
pick the project that I want
to export my billing data into,

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.290
the BigQuery data set
that I want to export--

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:38.110
San Francisco Bike Company.

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:39.763
I'll click Save.

00:12:39.763 --> 00:12:40.430
And there we go.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.560
Now it's enabled,
and every day it's

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.590
going to start syncing my
billing data into BigQuery

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:47.610
so I can do more
advanced stuff with that.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:51.410
So let's go to BigQuery and
see what that looks like.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.830
It roughly takes
a handful of hours

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:56.810
to get that first billing
export into BigQuery,

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:59.780
so what I've done is I
actually have a billing export

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.640
already in BigQuery itself.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.160
And if you look at
this schema, you

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.660
can see that it's capturing
some pretty decent information.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.510
So I have billing account IDs.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.360
You can see what service or
SKU I'm spending my money

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:14.290
on, projects, labels,
the specific location--

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:18.070
whether you're running your GCE
instance in Asia or the Us--

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.720
total cost, usage, credits,
all that kind of stuff.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.030
So some really good data.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.780
The challenge is that I want
to make this more insightful

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:29.680
and actually compare
it to my business KPIs.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.210
And so for my business data
set, or business table,

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:37.600
I have this SF Bike Trips
table in my data set.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.760
So by the way, this
SF Bike Trips table

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.460
is just essentially a
copy of the public data

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.440
set for the San Francisco Bike.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:46.990
So by all means, feel free
to explore this yourself

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.880
and have at it.

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:51.670
For this data, essentially
what I'm capturing

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.560
is information on
every single trip

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.060
that my customers are taking.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:57.100
So I have the total
duration of the trips,

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.280
when the trip started
and stopped and where.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:04.345
I have information on whether
that customer was a subscriber,

00:14:04.345 --> 00:14:05.170
i.e.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:08.020
like a monthly paid
membership, or just someone

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:12.010
who walked off the street and
took a ride on my bicycle.

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:19.950
And so what I'll do essentially
is join these two tables

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.060
with a simple SQL query.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:24.980
I think everyone
can still see that.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:28.328
And so essentially
what I'll do is a join

00:14:28.328 --> 00:14:29.370
between these two tables.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.247
So I have this SF Bike
Trips table first off,

00:14:32.247 --> 00:14:33.830
and what I'm pulling
out of this table

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.288
is the relevant
information for me.

00:14:35.288 --> 00:14:37.010
So I have the number
of trips, where

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:40.820
I'm counting the total number of
trips per day, total duration,

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.145
the date itself.

00:14:42.145 --> 00:14:43.520
I'm counting
whether the customer

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.970
was a subscriber or just
an off the street customer.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:51.007
And then I'm joining that
to my billing export.

00:14:51.007 --> 00:14:53.340
And from my billing export,
all I'm doing is pulling out

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.813
the particular day and
the total cost per day.

00:14:55.813 --> 00:14:57.730
So you can see that this
is relatively simple,

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:01.710
but you can take this to the
next level of potentially which

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:06.030
areas are the most profitable
for me with this bike data.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.370
That might tell
me maybe I should

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.280
start placing more bikes
in a particular area

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.110
or remove bikes
from other areas.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:16.718
It might break in to
things like maintenance.

00:15:16.718 --> 00:15:18.510
And I could do joins
across multiple tables

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:21.280
and identify, OK, I have
a team of five people who

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.530
do maintenance on these bikes.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:24.697
Where do I need to send
them to be a little bit more

00:15:24.697 --> 00:15:26.500
strategic with this?

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.730
And so I'll go ahead
and click Run Query.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.820
And you can see--

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:39.760
once it finishes-- you can see
that the query has broken out

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.500
the total number of trips--

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:42.940
I'll just hide this--

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.850
total number of trips and
compared it to the cost.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:49.600
So on average I have somewhere
between 2,000 and 4,000 trips

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.070
per day.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:54.280
My subscriber numbers
seem to be pretty healthy.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.130
My total cost per day-- once
again, totally fictitious-- is,

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.230
I don't know, somewhere
between $20 and $30 per day.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:01.250
So it looks pretty good.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:05.020
However, the challenge is that
to my just normal human eyes,

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:07.863
I can't really spot trends in
this data, so what I would do

00:16:07.863 --> 00:16:09.530
is actually open this
up in Data Studio.

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:12.460
So there's this fantastic
Explore in Data Studio button

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:16.002
that's going to open up a
new Data Studio project.

00:16:16.002 --> 00:16:18.460
But let me just show you the
one that I've already created.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:21.572
This took about five minutes,
so it's incredibly easy.

00:16:21.572 --> 00:16:23.780
And what I have is a couple
of different data points,

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:26.650
so first, let's look at this
cost versus KPI dashboard

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:27.520
up here.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.350
The blue line you can see are
the total number of trips.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:35.560
And so there seems to be a
pretty common trend here where

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:38.380
maybe bikes get ridden
more during the work days

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.000
and then less
during the weekends.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.860
People are riding their
own bikes, stuff like that,

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:44.193
hanging out with their kids,
et cetera, on the weekends.

00:16:44.193 --> 00:16:45.340
Makes sense.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:48.712
And you can see that the red
bars are my total cost per day.

00:16:48.712 --> 00:16:50.170
Now, what I didn't
tell you earlier

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:53.110
is that I've actually started
a new initiative to find out

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.900
if I can adopt more bike
riders with let's just call it

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.190
a mobile app.

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:59.320
And so I started
spending more money

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:03.460
on this mobile application
somewhere around mid-February.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:07.030
And you can see that my costs
have increased, however,

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:08.859
the blue line, the
total number of trips,

00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:10.685
hasn't really increased.

00:17:10.685 --> 00:17:12.310
In fact, there's some
interesting dips.

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:13.685
I mean, potentially,
maybe it was

00:17:13.685 --> 00:17:14.960
rain or something like that.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.710
But right out of the
gate, you can kind of

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:20.920
see that maybe this business
initiative isn't really

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.313
as successful as I
first planned it to be.

00:17:23.313 --> 00:17:24.730
And so I can look
at these numbers

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.790
down here below, which kind
of summarize the total numbers

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:28.560
across the month.

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.190
And this actually
proves my point in

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:32.830
that GCB costs per
month have risen

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:36.740
by 55%, kind of a warning sign
unless the bike trips have also

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:37.240
increased.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:42.430
But they're actually down by
6.2%, so a little concerning.

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:45.520
The subscribers are also
down, and my non-subscribers

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.290
have only increased by 1%

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:51.190
So right off the bat, I can
take this data to my business,

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.850
to CFO or CTO et
cetera, and kind of

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:59.230
show him that maybe this trend,
or this business initiative,

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:02.510
really isn't panning
out as much as possible.

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:06.700
So this is more of a kind
of theoretical exercise,

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:08.740
more of like the book smarts.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:11.242
I'd like to invite
Elad Amit onstage

00:18:11.242 --> 00:18:13.450
to talk through more of the
street smarts of actually

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:17.930
putting this into practice
and all the business value

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.710
you are able to contribute
back to PerimeterX.

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.300
ELAD AMIT: Thank you very much.

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:24.940
Thanks for that
great intro, Nick.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.340
Like you said, my
name is Elad Amit.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:31.150
I'm the director of platform
development over at PerimeterX,

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:35.120
where my team is primarily in
charge of scaling our product.

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:39.220
And one of the things that
entails is cost management.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:41.650
Just for a context,
what we actually do

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:43.480
is something called
bot mitigation.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.600
That is, we ingest a
boatload of traffic.

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:49.990
We analyze it, and
we use it to block

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:53.800
all sorts of malicious traffic,
stuff like account abuse,

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.570
account takeover,
credit card theft,

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:58.270
all sorts of nasty
little things.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.790
And the problem is that
a boatload of traffic

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:04.030
costs a boatload of money, so
naturally we try and give it

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:05.920
the attention it deserves.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:10.240
Now, I know you were
promised a demo,

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:14.152
but instead, what I'm going
to do is tell you a story,

00:19:14.152 --> 00:19:14.860
switch to a demo.

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:17.440
It's really a demo.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:21.100
Now, like every good
story, we have characters,

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.250
and our specific story
starts with our CFO, who

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:29.860
was actually quite angry with us
at the time, and rightfully so.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:31.600
I mean, all he
wanted to know was

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.362
what our cloud bills
are going to be

00:19:33.362 --> 00:19:34.570
like at the end of the month.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:39.310
And though that may sound
like a trivial question,

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:40.690
this was like two years ago.

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:43.480
And two years ago,
what we mostly had

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:46.900
was old flat file export
that looked kind of like this

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:49.990
with very, very
little dimensions

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.630
that we could kind of figure
out what we were doing.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:57.250
And though, as Nick showed
you a second ago, these days

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.820
you already have these
nifty little projections

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:02.800
that you can understand
what's going on,

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:04.540
at the time they didn't exist.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:11.620
For us, the real turning point
was when we started using DoiT

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:15.280
and their tool called
reOptimize to kind of start

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:20.800
figuring out a little bit better
where we're going to be at.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:24.400
This is specifically reOptimize
with some projections

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:26.800
that you can see here.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:32.770
At that point, our CFO was
quite happy, but as we grew

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:35.800
and as we matured,
people started asking

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.020
some really weird questions.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:38.800
I mean, up till
then, people asked,

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.750
how much is stuff going to cost?

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:42.580
And at that point
in time, people

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:47.090
started asking, how much
is my stuff going to cost?

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:48.790
This totally blew our minds.

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:55.630
So as we go into
the billing export

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:58.540
that Nick showed you
a second ago, what

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:02.890
you can see, at the default
that you're getting,

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:09.803
is mostly semantics based on
what Google is selling you.

00:21:09.803 --> 00:21:11.470
I'm not saying that
as a negative thing,

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:13.550
because that's actually
what you're paying for.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:17.110
But if you can see here,
what you're mostly seeing

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.120
is stuff like custom instance,
yadda, yadda, yadda, containers

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:21.550
this, active storage that.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:24.460
And making the leap
from that to whatever it

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.850
is that you're building
is somewhere between

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:30.140
hard and impossible.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:34.360
Now thankfully enough,
at that point--

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.670
that this was like a
year and a half ago--

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:40.480
the billing export started
getting the user defined labels

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:44.020
pushed into them, and
surprisingly enough they

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:45.370
were always there.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:47.530
When we were using them
mostly for operational

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:50.200
stuff to kind of
break down what we

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.360
were using within production.

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:54.610
And not surprisingly
enough, we wanted

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.190
to use the same breakdown
to kind of figure

00:21:57.190 --> 00:21:59.080
out where our costs were going.

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:03.790
And to kind of simplify
our lives a little bit,

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:09.160
we chose to expose this
additional data using a view,

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:13.480
mostly because we wanted to
take away the complexity--

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:16.330
and you'll see the complexity a
little further down the line--

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.580
of how to figure out which
specific dimensions are really

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:20.830
interesting and which are not.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.540
Now specifically
within this view,

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:27.550
you're going to want to notice
two things, the first of which

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:31.210
is that we're exporting a
column called Partition Time.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:35.020
This basically allows the
BigQuery query, or Data Studio

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:39.490
querying BigQuery, to
prune the partitions

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:41.920
and make your queries
a little bit cheaper.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:45.950
For us the billing data set is
already around two terabytes,

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.375
so it adds up, and
it makes sense.

00:22:48.375 --> 00:22:50.500
And the second thing you're
going to want to notice

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.450
is that we're
trying to take away

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:56.320
the complexity of
un-nesting repeated fields.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:57.790
If you're going
to be using this,

00:22:57.790 --> 00:23:00.280
I highly recommend you
read up on un-nested fields

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:03.590
because you can make really,
really bad mistakes with them.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:05.500
So what we have
here specifically

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:09.070
is a summary of the amount of
credits and the specific label

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:11.180
that we want to use.

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:14.660
And if we go back to
our Data Studio reports,

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:19.240
then we can see that once
we break down our cost based

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:21.580
on this new billing
component that we've added,

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.070
then the semantics
you change completely

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:27.940
from what Google is selling to
what we are actually building.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:30.580
Here we can see how much the
specific front end component

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:33.760
is costing us, how much
this specific Elasticsearch

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.110
cluster is costing us.

00:23:35.110 --> 00:23:37.480
And this isn't just
the cores or the RAM

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:41.020
or any specific piece,
it's everything.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:44.080
The other nifty little thing
that you get when you add this

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:49.150
is that you can now not
only see the overall spend,

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:51.610
or just by SKU or by component.

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:55.930
Once you break down a specific
component by the SKUs,

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:58.840
then you can figure out where
to focus your development

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:01.450
efforts on optimization,
which is with this whole talk

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:02.570
is about.

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:04.960
So if you take, for example,
that online component, then

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:09.442
we can see that it is mostly
a CPU intensive resource.

00:24:09.442 --> 00:24:11.650
You can see this is Custom
Instance Core in America--

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.290
it's out of Virginia if
I remember correctly--

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:15.740
and so on and so forth.

00:24:15.740 --> 00:24:18.850
And to contrast that, we
have this little thing, which

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:22.930
is mostly a network
intensive resource.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:26.830
Now another thing to
notice is that, while you

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.590
would have probably gotten this
from operational dashboards,

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:31.480
you would have not
gotten the fact

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:35.445
that it is using network, which
is that much more expensive.

00:24:35.445 --> 00:24:36.820
For those of you
who have already

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:39.800
been using GCP, or pretty
much any other cloud provider,

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:43.300
you know that not all
networking costs the same.

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:45.190
Some of it costs nothing.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:49.000
This specific thing costs
a lot, so naturally we

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.750
wanted to optimize it.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:54.400
And if we go further
down the line,

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:59.740
you can also see that throughout
roughly the last five to six

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:02.170
months we've actually
focused our development

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:06.160
efforts on bringing
this network cost down.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.977
And you can see how this nice
little line keeps dropping

00:25:08.977 --> 00:25:10.060
and dropping and dropping.

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:13.900
And this kept making our
CFO happier and happier,

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:15.520
which is a lot of my job.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:21.040
Now somewhere around
this point in time,

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.690
you're probably going to try
and squint and figure out

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:26.620
whether all these lines really
mean what I say they meant.

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:30.530
And surprisingly enough, we
had the exact same problem.

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:31.930
It's called cognitive load.

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.630
When you look at a graph
with 50 lines trying

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:38.760
to explain what your networking
cost is, another 80 trying

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:41.205
to explain what you're
a CPU costs are like,

00:25:41.205 --> 00:25:42.580
you're going to
have a hard time.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:47.170
And graphs should
be easily read.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:50.470
At this point, we're going
to jump back to our marvelous

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:53.080
little view, and we're going
to augment it with a case

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:55.900
class, specifically this.

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:57.850
Now, what we were
doing here actually

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:00.670
is we were taking the different
SKUs and trying to group

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:02.980
them into slightly
more coherent groups.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:07.180
What you'll see here is various
commitments, preemptible cores,

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:10.150
RAM, and all sorts
of networking stuff.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:15.790
We chose to do this and not
join it with a lookup table

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:19.680
mostly because we have zero
control over the actual SKUs

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.180
going into the system, because
they're controlled by Google.

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:23.847
And throughout the
last couple of years,

00:26:23.847 --> 00:26:25.515
they have changed
every once in a while.

00:26:25.515 --> 00:26:26.890
And what was more
important to us

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:30.130
was to have these groups
as stable as possible.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:35.170
And now, if we go back here
and we look at the graph

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:37.240
by this nifty little
SKU group, then you

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:40.090
can see that, indeed, I was
not lying through my teeth

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:43.300
and that the main cost--

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.700
insanely so-- is this
purple little line

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:49.510
that kept going down and
down and down, again, making

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:51.760
a lot of people happy.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:54.130
At this point in
time, you're probably

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:59.080
going to try and ask me what
happened during October.

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:00.880
And-- sneak preview--

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:02.590
we did not get a
bunch of coupons

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:06.640
that we entered into the
system dropping our costs.

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:09.710
About two weeks before
this point in time,

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:12.670
we got a lot of people starting
to complain about how they

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:14.040
were losing the bigger picture.

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.960
And, you know,
they're quite right.

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:20.660
We went from very
coarse kind of SKU-

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:23.085
and product-based
information all the way down

00:27:23.085 --> 00:27:24.460
to the specific
billing component

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:26.420
with nothing in between.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:28.360
So at that point
in time, naturally,

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:32.440
we started adding more and
more labels onto our resources

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:33.800
to get more information.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:37.978
Unfortunately, we kind of
missed this little piece

00:27:37.978 --> 00:27:39.520
of the documentation,
which basically

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:44.620
says that once you cross the
1,000 unique label value line,

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:47.360
it starts discarding them.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:50.800
So the end result was a
complete loss of visibility

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:53.680
because it kept the labels
that we weren't actually

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.260
interested in.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:59.470
Now obviously, we fixed it,
but being aggressive people,

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:01.180
we wanted to both
have what we wanted

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:03.250
and still have the
whole thing work.

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:05.290
So we went back
to our view again.

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:08.290
Have I mentioned how
much I like this view?

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:10.430
And here we decided--

00:28:10.430 --> 00:28:14.470
let me just make this a
little bit more readable.

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:19.870
Here we decided to join that
building component that we had

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:23.295
with a slightly
smaller data set,

00:28:23.295 --> 00:28:25.420
which is just going to be
those enrichments that we

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:26.350
added before.

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:28.570
Since they were
relatively kind of static,

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:31.497
for those of you in the
data warehouse business,

00:28:31.497 --> 00:28:33.580
this is kind of like a
star schema but not really.

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:38.550
So here we added stuff
like which product

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.320
this specific component
was serving, which service,

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:43.450
the few general details--

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:45.970
whether it was production or
non-production, whether it

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:48.045
was manually scaled or
auto scaled, and a bunch

00:28:48.045 --> 00:28:49.420
of other stuff
that I'm not going

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:54.140
to mention here because I don't
want to dive too deep into it.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:58.180
And then if we again
go back, we can

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:02.920
see that we can now bunch
stuff up into those--

00:29:02.920 --> 00:29:06.340
let's call them uber services.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:09.160
And the difference
here in terms of what

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.110
you can do with your
development effort

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:14.950
is that if you usually looked
at just a single component, then

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:18.100
you're going to be doing
some local optimizations.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:21.620
Here you can look at a
much broader spectrum.

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:23.920
And for example, let's
imagine that this kind of

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:28.345
said for your live front end, or
API, or something of the like.

00:29:28.345 --> 00:29:30.430
And here you can start
thinking about slightly

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:33.550
more cross cutting changes,
like using a different protocol

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:39.300
or looking at a different
processing mechanism.

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:45.980
Now at this point in time--
this was, I don't know,

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:47.700
two months ago,
three months ago--

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:48.510
we were ecstatic.

00:29:48.510 --> 00:29:49.440
Everything was great.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.460
We were really, really happy.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.870
And then this came along.

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:59.070
Needless to say, a
13% increase in costs

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:02.010
is not a good thing for
anyone and really, really

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.890
makes most people
angry, especially when

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:09.570
you keep telling them that
everything is improving.

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:11.010
Or is it?

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:14.310
And the problematic
part is that life

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:17.100
doesn't exist in a vacuum,
especially your costs.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.360
And at that point in time, we
were growing at an insane rate.

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:24.270
And we didn't want to be
the guys who kind of raised

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:27.750
the wrong flag, so we
want it to kind improve

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.490
how we were looking
at things and try

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:33.960
to take out that noise
within the signal

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.020
that we were looking at.

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.380
And the way that
we chose to do this

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:41.700
is start looking at
relative figures instead

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:43.680
of the absolutes.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.630
If you remember, up till
now, we looked at dollars,

00:30:46.630 --> 00:30:50.190
and what we decided to look
at was the relative cost

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:56.080
of pushing a million page views
or requests through our system,

00:30:56.080 --> 00:31:01.580
and by that kind of try and take
out the traffic fluctuations.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:02.800
The way that we did that--

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:06.610
back to our marvelous
view-- was, once again,

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:10.960
joining the billing data
with some more business

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:14.140
metrics that were already there
because we run a business,

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.860
and we need to know how much
traffic is coming through.

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:20.440
And once we took
this information

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:26.330
and moved back to Data
Studios, you can see--

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:29.930
or what all of you
can do as well--

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:35.210
you can add something called a
calculated field, in this case,

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:37.580
something quite simple.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:39.970
In case you're not
being able to see this,

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:44.720
this is the final cost divided
by the amount of traffic.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:48.430
And then when you
look at it, things

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:51.370
start to make
slightly more sense.

00:31:51.370 --> 00:31:54.220
Over here you can see that,
as I mentioned before,

00:31:54.220 --> 00:31:55.210
everything is fine.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:59.530
Cost is indeed going up,
but our cost efficiencies

00:31:59.530 --> 00:32:00.820
are going down.

00:32:00.820 --> 00:32:04.750
Again, this makes most of
the people happy on our end.

00:32:04.750 --> 00:32:07.750
Just as an example, if
we take another look

00:32:07.750 --> 00:32:10.220
at the service we
looked at before,

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:15.430
this is the same cost efficiency
metric for that service.

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:18.340
And you can see that
indeed it has gone down,

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:22.540
with this day being
a party that we threw

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:24.100
when we went down that low.

00:32:26.616 --> 00:32:31.450
To kind of try and
summarize our experiences

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:34.900
from the last couple
of years, I think

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:37.660
what I want to leave you with
is three things, the first

00:32:37.660 --> 00:32:41.090
of which is label everything.

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.060
And when I say everything,
I mean everything--

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:45.880
at least once, probably
not more than once

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:48.350
because that'll be bad.

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:50.483
But even if you don't
see the value in it now,

00:32:50.483 --> 00:32:52.150
you're really going
to want to label it,

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:54.820
because further down the
line when stuff does grow,

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:56.260
you're going to
lose that history.

00:32:56.260 --> 00:32:59.050
And that never comes back.

00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:02.260
The second is that human
beings are inherently good.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:05.878
And when you show
them this information,

00:33:05.878 --> 00:33:07.420
you're probably
going to be surprised

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:09.215
with the amount of
negative emotions

00:33:09.215 --> 00:33:11.320
that they're are
going to throw at you.

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:13.330
What we've found is
that this is mostly

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:19.480
because it's a muscle most
people do not flex that often.

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:22.840
I know a lot of societies
that teach their children how

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:26.710
to manage budgets well
or deal with money,

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:31.300
and the more that
you connect the money

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:33.640
end of things to what
they're actually building

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:37.360
and what they're good
at and practice a lot,

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:38.860
for example, the
building components

00:33:38.860 --> 00:33:40.875
or the actual traffic
and the business metrics,

00:33:40.875 --> 00:33:42.250
then the more
they're going to be

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:44.080
willing to take
this as something

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:45.640
that they're in charge of.

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:49.150
And the third, SQL
is your friend.

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:51.100
A billing invoice
for us right now

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:53.920
is somewhere between six
and seven million lines

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:55.250
in the billing export.

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:59.080
There's no way that you're going
to be able to understand what's

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:02.290
going on there without
reporting in aggregations,

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:05.240
and that's what SQL is for.

00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:08.510
And with that, I turn
the mic back to Nick.

00:34:08.510 --> 00:34:10.900
NICK ORLOVE: And can we
switch back to the slides?

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:13.750
So, Elad, you're absolutely
right, making your CFO happy

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:15.100
is critical.

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:19.840
I'm sure everyone has to kind
of figure that out themselves.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:22.900
Besides your three takeaways, I
also want to add in my three--

00:34:22.900 --> 00:34:25.750
or four takeaways actually for
best practices for everyone

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:28.630
who has graciously
attended the session.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:33.760
First being, on day one, not
day 30, day 90, on day one,

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:37.719
start exporting your
billing data into BigQuery

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:40.090
so that you can
start building costs

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:41.949
analysis dashboards
so you can actually

00:34:41.949 --> 00:34:44.120
have that data in
the first place

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:45.909
so that when,
potentially, problems

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:50.139
arise, when you spend increases
beyond what you're expecting,

00:34:50.139 --> 00:34:52.600
you have that data so that
you can actually go back in,

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:53.929
do some analysis.

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:55.429
You know exactly
where that data is.

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:57.400
You already have your
dashboards, all that stuff,

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:59.020
instead of, in the
heat of the moment,

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:01.950
trying to collect that data,
figure out where all of it is,

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:03.700
and then run through
all these SQL queries

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:04.880
and stuff like that.

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:08.040
So start capturing
it on day one.

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:11.210
Second this is
structure and organize

00:35:11.210 --> 00:35:14.810
your projects, your
organizations, your folders,

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:17.090
your labels, all that
stuff, across all

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:18.950
of your different GCP resources.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:23.150
So, Elad, you're 100% right,
by just throwing everything

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:27.140
into one single project
or not using labels,

00:35:27.140 --> 00:35:30.410
doesn't really give you any
kind of fine-grained control

00:35:30.410 --> 00:35:32.960
over what you're spending
on and how those spends

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:34.950
are affecting your
business, et cetera.

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.340
So make sure that
you start actually

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:42.980
using resource hierarchies,
labeling assets, putting items

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:46.320
into projects, into
folders, all that stuff.

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:49.530
Third is make sure
that you're configuring

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:52.500
the right kind of policies
for the right kind of people

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:55.920
who have access to control
costs, to set budgets,

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:58.030
to set alerts, all
that kind of stuff.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:36:00.480
So rather than just giving
free reign to everyone

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:03.000
to start spending as
much as they want,

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:05.550
maybe you have department
heads or something like that,

00:36:05.550 --> 00:36:07.980
or use things like
Google Groups,

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:10.110
or the production
team has more control

00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:12.490
over spending habits
than the dev test team,

00:36:12.490 --> 00:36:15.780
something like that, so that
spend doesn't go out of control

00:36:15.780 --> 00:36:18.090
and you start trying to
scramble and find out

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.070
what happened when.

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:23.785
And fourth is building a
culture of showback in your org.

00:36:23.785 --> 00:36:26.160
I've talked to a bunch of
different customers about this.

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:28.220
Some customers
call it shameback.

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:31.140
I'll kind of let you guys
decide on which culture

00:36:31.140 --> 00:36:34.410
fit is right for your
company, but essentially,

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:37.530
the ability to regularly
review these billing reports

00:36:37.530 --> 00:36:39.480
and custom dashboards
with your teams,

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:41.800
with your business
units, et cetera.

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:44.520
And then regularly setting
up budgets and alerts so

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:46.980
that you can closely
monitor your costs,

00:36:46.980 --> 00:36:50.148
and you can alert stakeholders
when costs start to increase,

00:36:50.148 --> 00:36:52.190
things start to become
concerning, what have you,

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:54.678
so that you can catch
the train way before it

00:36:54.678 --> 00:36:55.470
leaves the station.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:58.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

